  "text": "We are asked to determine the number of **4-digit numbers** such that:\n\n> The **sum of the squares of the first two digits** equals the **sum of the squares of the last two digits**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the structure of a 4-digit number\n\nA 4-digit number has the form:\n\n$$\n\\overline{abcd} = 1000a + 100b + 10c + d\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $ a \\in \\{1, 2, ..., 9\\} $ (since it's the first digit of a 4-digit number, it can't be 0)\n- $ b, c, d \\in \\{0, 1, ..., 9\\} $\n\nWe are told that:\n\n$$\na^2 + b^2 = c^2 + d^2\n$$\n\nWe need to count all such 4-digit numbers that satisfy this condition.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Strategy\n\nWe can iterate over all possible values of $ a, b, c, d $ that satisfy:\n\n- $ a \\in [1, 9] $\n- $ b, c, d \\in [0, 9] $\n- $ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 + d^2 $\n\nRather than checking all $ 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 9000 $ combinations, we can instead:\n\n- Precompute all possible values of $ x = a^2 + b^2 $ for $ a \\in [1, 9], b \\in [0, 9] $\n- For each value of $ x $, count how many $ (a, b) $ pairs give that sum\n- Then, for each value of $ x $, count how many $ (c, d) $ pairs give that sum\n- Then the number of 4-digit numbers for that $ x $ is:\n\n$$\n\\text{count}(a, b) \\times \\text{count}(c, d)\n$$\n\nSum this over all possible $ x $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Precompute all possible sums of squares\n\nWe'll compute the set of all possible values of $ a^2 + b^2 $, where $ a \\in [1,9], b \\in [0,9] $.\n\nLet’s compute this:\n\nWe can write a small table of all possible $ a^2 + b^2 $ values.\n\nBut",
